 ### Fixed
 
+- `onError` hook now fires for rejections that are not `Error` instances, wrapping them in an `Error` with the original value preserved as the `cause`. A provider that rejects with a string, plain object, or other non-Error value will now trigger `onError`, whereas it previously bypassed the hook silently. The re-thrown value at the caller boundary remains unchanged — the caller still receives the original rejection unchanged. **Migration note**: if your error counting or monitoring relies on `onError` being called, it will now count a class of failures that were previously silently dropped.
 - `onError` and `afterSession` hooks now fire when `beforeSession` throws, matching the guarantee the hook names imply. Previously, a throw from `beforeSession` escaped the `try`/`finally` block entirely, so neither hook ran. **Migration note**: if your `afterSession` hook assumed it would only be called after a full session turn, it will now also be called when `beforeSession` itself fails — the hook can inspect the session to detect this case (no entries beyond the initial user message will have been processed).
 
